<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the first of the five projects: "grain interfaces, resource models,
      the JSON Schemas". This one is four schemas, the four action shapes, and the pure functions that
      turn a desired body into the wire types CyberCloud.Communication's grains hold.

      ⚠ NO GRAIN INTERFACES OF ITS OWN, AND FOR A REASON NO PROVIDER BEFORE IT COULD GIVE. Every
      provider so far said "everything a reconcile needs is in the desired body ResourceGrain already
      stores". This one converges onto grains that ALREADY EXIST — ICommunicationServiceGrain,
      IMessageTemplateGrain, ISuppressionListGrain in CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts, wired into
      the silo host since before this family was written, because identity's OTPs and monitor's alerts
      go through them. The provider is the tenant-facing face of that module, not a second copy of it.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts, WHICH NO PROVIDER MAY DO WITHOUT A LINE IN
      module-layering.txt — and the line is there, with its reason. What a provider may reference is
      "CyberCloud.Core, CyberCloud.Kubernetes, CyberCloud.ResourceManager and CyberCloud.Tenancy, and
      nothing else" (src/Providers/README.md § Hard rule); this family is the one exception, because
      the resource types docs/plan/17 § CyberCloud.Communication/services describes ARE that module's
      grains, and CyberCloud.Identity -> CyberCloud.Communication already forbids moving the grains
      into a provider (identity may not reference one).

      ⚠ NO CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Contracts. This is the first clusterless family in the catalogue —
      docs/plan/08 § What the resource manager deliberately does not do names "a DNS zone, a mail
      domain, a role assignment" as the shape — and nothing here renders an object, so nothing here
      names a kind.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts/CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts/CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
